PLACES=[
N('ludington-house-lawrence-ks','Ludington House, Lawrence','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.95417,-95.23972,'site','view','drive','','1860–1872',
  'The front of the house was built in 1860 to a design by the Kansas architect John G. Haskell for Reuben W. Ludington, a Lawrence businessman and civic leader. Solon O. Thacher, who had chaired the 1859 Wyandotte convention that wrote the Kansas constitution, bought it in 1869. In 1872 the house was remodelled and the whole back half, with a round tower and a porte-cochere, was added.',
  'A tall, busy outline of one to three stories with round and square towers, seven brick chimneys, two balconies on the east front and a long wooden porch at the back. The 1971 Register form calls the walls red brick with light stone quoins on the corners of the 1860 section and describes cherry woodwork, gold-leaf mouldings, embossed leather wainscoting and ten fireplaces inside; it was then a private home restored by owners who bought it in 1959. Whether the inside is still as described was not checked. The caption of a 2009 photograph on Wikimedia Commons says the broken tower windows in it came from a fire; the present condition was not checked.',
  'Haskell\'s villa of 1860 with a tower added in 1872, on Tennessee Street in Old West Lawrence.',
  'Private house at 1613 Tennessee Street; look from the sidewalk and do not go onto the grounds. Park on the street. Its use today and whether it ever opens to visitors were not checked.',
  [['National Register form, 1971',NR%'71000310'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%7]],
  brick='yes'),
N('roberts-castle-lawrence-ks','John N. Roberts House ("The Castle"), Lawrence','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.96000,-95.23640,'exact','view','drive','','1893–1894',
  'A castle-like stone mansion at 13th and Massachusetts, built for John N. Roberts, a Lawrence manufacturer who made his money from patents on wooden butter and lard containers. It went up during the financial panic of 1893 to 1894 and was finished in the fall of 1894 (the architect was John Haskell). A seventeen-year-old visitor, Sidney Endacott, was hired to carve the mantels, fireplaces and newel posts, and later became a noted sculptor in England. The Roberts family lived here until 1919. After Libby Kriz-Fiorito bought it in 1947 it became the Castle Tea Room; her foundation restored it, and it reopened in May 2009.',
  'A fifteen-room, three-storey Romanesque house of ashlar limestone with a massive corner tower, a slate and lead roof and carved stonework at the porch and windows. The sources disagree on the stone: the Register form says it came from Cottonwood Falls and gives another version that it was quarried locally. Inside, the 1974 form describes five fireplaces below and four above, each room finished in a different wood, and etched glass in the vestibule; a 2009 article says the restoration kept the woodwork and restored the stained-glass windows.',
  'A limestone castle in the middle of Lawrence, with the carving of a teenage sculptor inside.',
  'Private property at 1307 Massachusetts Street; look from the street. A 2009 Journal-World article said it was then used for private events and meant to open for small public events such as tea parties; whether it takes visitors now was not checked, so no hours are given. Street parking; the nearest mapped lot is on West 13th Street.',
  [['National Register form, 1974',NR%'74000831'],['Lawrence Journal-World, 2009: the Castle Tea Room reopens','https://www2.ljworld.com/news/2009/jul/05/new-reig-lawrence-landmark-castle-tea-room-open-ag/'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%10]],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('blood-house-lawrence-ks','Col. James Blood House, Lawrence','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.96500,-95.23940,'site','view','drive','','about 1870',
  'A red brick house of about 1870 at 1015 Tennessee Street, one of the oldest houses left in Lawrence. It is named for James Blood, who came to Kansas Territory in 1854 as an agent of the New England Emigrant Aid Company, helped pick the site of Lawrence and guided the first settlers to it on August 1, 1854. He was the town\'s first mayor in 1857, a delegate to the 1859 Wyandotte convention and a trustee of the school that became the University of Kansas. The form says the builder is not recorded; Blood was listed as a bricklayer in 1876 and may have done some of the work.',
  'Two storeys with solid red clay brick walls twelve inches thick on a limestone foundation, tall first-floor windows that the 1972 form thinks hold the original glass and shutters, a hipped tin roof, paired brackets under the eaves and an ornate front porch with paired columns on pedestals. The form calls the outside basically unchanged from Blood\'s day. Not checked since.',
  'The house of the man who led the first settlers to the site of Lawrence.',
  'Private house at 1015 Tennessee Street (a private residence when the form was written in 1972); look from the sidewalk. Street parking. Whether it ever opens to visitors was not checked.',
  [['National Register form, 1972',NR%'72000493'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary (Blood House listed 1972)','https://www.kansashistory.gov/natreg/natreg_listings/search/page:1/county:DG/records:all/submit:SEARCH']],
  brick='yes'),
N('priestly-house-lawrence-ks','Priestly House, Lawrence','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.95720,-95.23830,'site','view','drive','','1864–1886',
  'Built about 1864, in the building boom that followed Quantrill\'s raid of 1863, as a house of the upright-and-wing type that New Englanders and emigrants from east of the Mississippi brought west; the form calls it one of the few such Greek Revival houses left in Lawrence. William Priestly bought the property in 1872 and a wing was added in 1886. His family lived here until 1986. The house was then threatened with demolition for apartment buildings, and the Lawrence Preservation Alliance bought it to save it and sold it in 1987 with restrictions on changes to the outside.',
  'A symmetrical two-storey house facing Kentucky Street, with stretcher-bond brick walls on a rough limestone foundation, brick header courses over the lintels and deep-set windows. The brick of the original west wall was uncovered in 1954 and now forms an inside wall of an addition covered in aluminium siding, so only part of the house shows brick from outside. The 1988 form says the plan inside was altered, with the walnut stair rail milled on the site in 1886. Not checked since.',
  'One of the few early Greek Revival houses left in Lawrence, bought by a preservation group in 1986 to stop its demolition.',
  'Private house in the Oread neighborhood facing Kentucky Street; look from the sidewalk. Street parking. The Register form of 1988 is the only source read; ownership and use today were not checked.',
  [['National Register form, 1988',NR%'88000199'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%9]],
  brick='some'),
N('parmenter-hall-baker-university-baldwin-city-ks','Parmenter Hall, Baker University, Baldwin City','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.77834,-95.18787,'exact','view','drive','','1865–1871',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey limestone building with a mansard roof, begun on the Baker University campus in 1865 as the college\'s first permanent building. Baker, chartered in 1858, is the oldest four-year college in Kansas; its first building, Old Castle Hall, stands a block off campus. Money for Parmenter ran short: the cornerstone was laid in June 1866, but by 1869 only the foundation and first-floor walls were done, and a second Register form puts the finish at about 1871. President Lincoln gave $100 to the building fund in 1864.',
  'Native stone walls 80 by 60 feet, smaller stones on the first storey than the second and darker quoins on the corners, a mansard roof of wood shingles, a carved doorway with a curved pediment on the east front and a double stair to the basement. The open belfry on the roof was added after the building was finished. The 1977 form calls the style influenced by French Empire. Its use now and the inside were not checked.',
  'The oldest big building at the oldest college in Kansas, paid for in part by a hundred dollars from Lincoln.',
  'On the campus of Baker University, at Eighth and Dearborn Streets; look from the campus walks and ask at the university before going in. Street parking on Dearborn Street.',
  [['National Register form, 1977',NR%'77000579'],['National Register form for Case Library, 1986 (dates the hall 1865–1871)',NR%'86001232'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%9]],
  brick='no'),
N('case-library-baker-university-baldwin-city-ks','Case Library, Baker University, Baldwin City','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.77732,-95.18821,'exact','view','drive','','1904–1907',
  'Baker University\'s first library building, a two-storey neoclassical limestone building on Eighth Street. Judge Nelson E. Case of Oswego, a trustee, gave $8,000 in 1898 to start it, and in 1906 Andrew Carnegie added $25,000, with a promise of $12,000 more for books if the university raised an endowment. The whole cost about $52,000. Its four-storey steel stacks, with glass floors, held 50,000 volumes.',
  'Rusticated yellowish limestone walls on a raised foundation of larger grey-tan blocks, a pedimented central pavilion and whitish cut-stone belt courses. The 1986 form says the outside kept its character and the inside had been greatly altered. Its use today was not checked.',
  'A Carnegie-funded college library of 1907, with a stone front on Baldwin City\'s main street.',
  'On the campus of Baker University at Eighth and Grove Streets, facing Eighth Street; look from the public sidewalk. Street parking. Whether the public may go in was not checked.',
  [['National Register form, 1986',NR%'86001232'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%2]],
  brick='no'),
N('vermilya-boener-house-lawrence-ks','Vermilya-Boener House, north of Lawrence','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.01560,-95.24250,'exact','view','drive','','1866–1868',
  'A two-storey Italian Villa house of rusticated limestone block built in 1866 to 1868 by the early settler Elijah Vermilya and his wife Cynthia. It later belonged to their daughter Ella and her husband William Boener, who owned the Boener Brothers cigar factory on Massachusetts Street, a major Lawrence employer around 1900. The family lived in it until 1948 and it has stood empty since the sale (the 1991 form says the mid-1950s; a 2010 newspaper says the family sold in 1948). The Kansas Preservation Alliance named it one of the state\'s most endangered historic places in 2010, when it stood on land a company had considered for a sand-dredging pit.',
  'The 1991 form describes a cross-hipped roof with four brick chimneys, stone walls about eighteen inches thick and tall windows, and says the inside had been gutted: partitions stripped to the studs, the main stair and some trim gone, and the exterior had lost its cornice and built-in gutter. A 2010 article says the windows were covered in plywood and that an earlier owner built a new roof and mothballed the house. Its condition today was not checked.',
  'An Italian Villa of Douglas County stone, abandoned for decades with the roof kept on.',
  'On private land about a mile north of US 24 on North 1900 Road, northwest of Lawrence. Do not go onto the property; look from the county road if it can be seen from there (not checked), and nothing here says the public may go in. Pull-offs were not checked. The pin is the Register\'s point, looked at on the aerial.',
  [['National Register form, 1991',NR%'91001961'],['Lawrence Journal-World, 2010: state group lists the villa as endangered','https://www2.ljworld.com/news/2010/mar/28/state-group-places-villa-list-endangered-historic/'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%12]],
  brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('stoebener-barn-worden-ks','Stoebener Barn, near Worden','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.77310,-95.34780,'area','view','drive','','1914',
  'A two-storey gable-roofed horse barn built about 1914 by John Stoebener Jr. on his family\'s homestead near Worden, with the stonework done by Lawrence Schmidt, a mason who lived southwest of Worden. The 1989 listing was for a barn type common in eastern Kansas between 1890 and 1920, with limestone walls below wood; the form says many of its neighbours have been covered in metal siding but this one keeps its original wood siding. The 1915 farm census lists six horses, twenty-two cattle and seventeen swine on the farm.',
  'Limestone walls on the lower level with vertical wood planking above, a loft floor carried on laminated planks and stone walls, and a king-post roof. The 1988 form said it was in good shape but needed its windows glazed and a problem in the stone wall fixed. A frame barn of about 1904 stands to the south-west. Not seen on the ground; its condition today is not checked.',
  'A stone-and-timber horse barn of 1914, one of the few in its area still in its original siding.',
  'On a private farm in Willow Springs Township, northwest of Worden; do not enter the farm. Whether it can be seen from a public road was not checked. The pin is the Register\'s point and may be a farm road away from the barn.',
  [['National Register form, 1988',NR%'88003083'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%11]],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('stony-point-lutheran-church-ks','Stony Point Evangelical Lutheran Church, near Vinland','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.82506,-95.21075,'exact','view','drive','','1882–1883',
  'A one-room frame church of 1882 to 1883 on a hilltop southwest of Vinland. The congregation began in 1860 as Christ\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church, one of the first Lutheran congregations in Kansas, with 24 charter members who met in the log house of Joseph Eberhart under the Rev. David Earhart. The church served the Eberhart and Hemphill families and others until the early 1900s. The Stony Point Evangelical Lutheran Church and Cemetery Association has kept it since 1898, and the 2006 form says it is used mainly on Memorial Day.',
  'A plain gable-front building, 24 by 42 feet, of wood lap siding on a limestone foundation, with eight windows and a double front door under a transom. The asbestos siding put on in 1956 was taken off in 2003 and the original wood repaired and painted in 2005. A cemetery of thirty-five plots lies south of the church. The 2006 form says the church is visible from North 600 Road, up a gravel lane, and sits inside a chain-link fence with a gate.',
  'A hilltop church and graveyard that opens, as far as the sources say, for Memorial Day.',
  'Private property at 1575 North 600 Road; look from the road and stay outside the fence. Interior not checked and no visiting hours found (the form says it is mostly used on Memorial Day). Whether anyone cares for it now was not checked.',
  [['National Register form, 2006',NR%'06001168'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%11]],
  brick='no'),
N('vinland-grange-hall-ks','Vinland Grange Hall, Vinland','Vinland, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.83920,-95.18220,'area','view','drive','','1884–1950',
  'A two-storey stuccoed limestone hall of about 1884 at Oak and Main Streets in Vinland, built for Vinland Grange No. 163, which was chartered in 1873 and was one of the oldest in Kansas. Like other grange halls of its day it had a meeting hall upstairs and a shop or farmers\' co-operative below. The grange used it for more than a hundred years, and when it stopped in 1985 it gave the building to the Santa Fe Trail Historical Society.',
  'Walls two feet thick of native limestone, laid in mortar made with sand hauled from the Kansas River at Lawrence in 1884, then stuccoed, with stone lintels and sills and a stair up the back. When the 2000 form was written the society was restoring the upper floor and the Kansas Grassroots Art Association used the ground floor as a museum. Whether it is open now was not checked.',
  'A limestone grange hall with a meeting room upstairs and a shop below.',
  'Corner of Oak and Main Streets in Vinland, on the village road; look from the street. Whether it opens to visitors, and the society\'s hours, could not be found, so none are given. Park on the roadside.',
  [['National Register form, 2000',NR%'00000037'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%12]],
  brick='no'),
N('vinland-presbyterian-church-ks','Vinland Presbyterian Church, Vinland','Vinland, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.84030,-95.18210,'exact','view','drive','','1879',
  'A small frame Gothic Revival church finished in 1879 on land given by the nurseryman William E. Barnes, called the "father of Vinland" for the lots he gave to the town. A Sunday School room was added in 1910. In the 1980s that room was turned into a one-and-a-half-storey house. Trains of the Santa Fe railroad passed in front of the church most of the years it was in use.',
  'A rectangular, front-gabled sanctuary with a steep roof, lancet windows, pointed double doors made of diagonal boards and a 50-foot steeple with an open belfry; the south roof slope still has wood shingles and a brick chimney. The 2003 form says the building is largely intact apart from the house conversion. The inside was not described in the part read.',
  'A white Gothic frame church from 1879 with a high steeple, built on land given by the man called the father of Vinland.',
  'On East 1725 Road in Vinland, with part now a private house; look from the road. No services or visiting hours found. Park on the roadside.',
  [['National Register form, 2003',NR%'03000707'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%13]],
  brick='some'),
N('quayle-house-baldwin-city-ks','William A. Quayle House, Baldwin City','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.78440,-95.18530,'site','view','drive','','1913–1921',
  'William A. Quayle (1860 to 1925) was a student and later president of Baker University, a Methodist minister and a bishop. He built this bungalow on three acres at the north edge of Baldwin City in 1913, added a larger Colonial Revival wing in 1921 and retired here in 1923. He landscaped the grounds as a small piece of wilderness, leaving stumps, dead trees and rotting logs where they lay.',
  'A weatherboard and shingle bungalow on a limestone foundation, joined to a Colonial Revival addition with oak Doric columns at the entry to a book-filled library; the 1995 form describes his small office off the library. Not checked on the ground; the grounds may have changed.',
  'The retirement house of a Methodist bishop who kept a wild garden.',
  'Private house at 210 North 6th Street; look from the street. Street parking. Whether it opens to visitors was not checked.',
  [['National Register form, 1994',NR%'94001624'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%9]],
  brick='no'),
N('henry-house-lecompton-ks','William Henry House, near Lecompton','Lecompton, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.01760,-95.43970,'site','view','drive','','1873',
  'A two-storey limestone farmhouse of 1873, built by William Henry, a Civil War veteran from Pennsylvania who came to Kansas in 1868 with his wife and two children. He built a large barn first, finished in 1869, and the stone house in 1873; an ell was added later, perhaps about 1879 when the family had grown to six. It was listed on the National Register in 2019 as a local example of a plain folk house.',
  'A T-shaped house of irregularly coursed limestone with corner quoins, arched stone window heads and dressed sills, and a five-bay south front reached by stone steps. A rock wall marks the east edge of the property, and the remains of the old barn stand across the road. The 2019 nomination says the house keeps its original look outside and in.',
  'A stone farmhouse that has barely changed since 1873, with a ruined barn across the road.',
  'Private farm at 344 North 1925 Road, about five miles southwest of Lecompton by the form; look from the road only. Whether the road gives a view of the house was not checked. The pin is the Register\'s point.',
  [['National Register nomination, listed 2019','https://www.kansashistory.gov/resource/national_register/nominationsNRDB/KS_DouglasCounty_HenryWilliamHouse_Listed%2009302019.pdf'],['Kansas Historical Society register listing',KSNR%5]],
  brick='no'),
N('lewis-crowder-cemetery-lecompton-ks','Lewis-Crowder Cemetery, near Lecompton','Lecompton, Kansas','ks','burial','marker',39.02220,-95.41020,'area','view','drive','','1879–1940',
  'A small burying ground started in 1879 by the family of Joshua and Mary Dent Lewis, used until 1940, with about 40 known burials of Black residents. The Lecompton Historical Society calls it the first African-American cemetery in Douglas County, and a 2019 county report calls it the only one still intact. The Kansas Historic Sites Board of Review approved its National Register nomination on February 7, 2026.',
  'A handful of grave markers in a field. Everything here comes from newspaper and county reports; the nomination itself was not read.',
  'The first Black burying ground in Douglas County, on private land.',
  'It is on private land between Lecompton and Lawrence, and nothing says the public may go in; the Lecompton Historical Society ran a guided visit after a panel talk in April 2026, so ask the society about the next one. The pin is the Register\'s point only and may be a field away from the graves.',
  [['Lawrence Journal-World, April 2026: panel on the cemetery\'s legacy','https://www2.ljworld.com/news/general-news/2026/apr/10/lecompton-historical-society-to-host-panel-discussion-about-legacy-of-african-american-cemetery-in-rural-douglas-county'],['Lawrence Journal-World, 2019: abandoned cemeteries','https://www2.ljworld.com/news/county-government/2019/oct/02/douglas-county-leaders-staff-discuss-responsibility-for-abandoned-cemeteries']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('clearfield-school-baldwin-city-ks','Clearfield School (District 58), northeast of Baldwin City','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.82629,-95.10061,'site','view','drive','','1900–1946',
  'A one-room country school built in 1900, at least the second schoolhouse of District 58. It was built a mile to the east and moved to its present site in 1908. The school closed in 1946 and the Clearfield Grange used it as a meeting house until the 1990s. When the 2014 listing was written the Clearfield Historical Society kept the building.',
  'The Register summary describes a Stick/Eastlake-style school on a half-acre site with two outhouses and a shed. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A one-room schoolhouse that was jacked up and moved a mile.',
  'On North 600 Road (2162 N 600 Rd); look from the road. Whether it opens, and when, was not checked.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%2]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('baldwin-city-school-ks','Baldwin City School and Auditorium-Gymnasium','Baldwin City, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.78049,-95.18811,'site','view','drive','','1923–1942',
  'The Baldwin City School, designed by Kansas City architect Charles A. Smith, opened in January 1923 and held both elementary and high school classes until a new high school was built in 1969. A detached auditorium and gymnasium designed by Topeka architect Thomas W. Williamson was finished in 1942 under the Work Projects Administration. It was listed on the National Register in 2015.',
  'Classical Revival and Moderne styles in two buildings on Chapel Street. Only the register summary was read; what each building is used for now was not checked.',
  'A 1923 school and its WPA gymnasium.',
  '704 Chapel Street, Baldwin City; look from the street. Use today not checked.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%1]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('knud-anderson-farmstead-vinland-ks','Knud Anderson Farmstead, Vinland','Vinland, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.84070,-95.15610,'site','view','drive','','1868–1972',
  'A farmstead just east of Vinland with a stone house, bank barn, smokehouse, granaries, garage and horse stable, the earliest building from 1868. The Kansas Historical Society says it is one of the oldest and most intact farmsteads in the area, and it was listed on the National Register in March 2023.',
  'Only the register summary was read, so no more detail is given. The stone house and bank barn are the main buildings.',
  'A stone-and-bank-barn farm that has kept its whole set of working buildings.',
  'Private farm at 1862 North 700 Road; look from the road. The pin is the Register\'s point.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%6]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
